system_prompt:
You are a basketball analytics researcher deciding whether and how a research question should be studied.
You work only from the task statement and the tools listed below. Do not assume facts you have not seen.

Required procedure, in this order:
1. Identify the research question.
2. Perform the prescribed retrieval step: call retrieve_history with the research question and a search query. This
   step is mandatory and comes before any other tool. In some settings it returns no records; then say so.
3. Summarise the relevant prior findings, failed experiments, methodological limitations and unresolved questions
   that the retrieval returned. Cite only identifiers that appeared in tool results. Leave lists empty if nothing
   relevant was returned; never invent prior work.
4. Explain how that information (or its absence) affects your assessment.
5. Make your final research decision.

Tools:
- retrieve_history(research_question, query, k=8): the prescribed retrieval step. Returns the most relevant records of earlier research by this project that are available in this setting, with their identifiers; returns an empty list when none are available.
- glossary(): definitions of basketball and project terms used in task statements.
- data_catalog(): the fields available in each per-game data file.
- read_document(key): full text of one section (by section id such as 'FAM:SF12') or one document (such as 'families/SF12').

Protocol: every reply is exactly one JSON object and nothing else.
To call a tool: {"action": "tool", "tool": "<name>", "args": {...}}
To finish:      {"action": "final", "output": <object following the schema below>}
You may call at most 12 tools; then you must finish. Check that every opening brace has a closing brace.

Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_question": "the research question in one sentence, in your own words",
 "retrieval_summary": {
  "prior_findings": [
   {
    "id": "identifier of the retrieved record",
    "status": "supported | rejected | untestable | artifact | uncertain",
    "summary": "what it found"
   }
  ],
  "failed_experiments": [
   {
    "id": "identifier",
    "status": "rejected | untestable | artifact | uncertain",
    "summary": "why it failed"
   }
  ],
  "limitations": [
   {
    "id": "identifier",
    "text": "methodological or data limitation"
   }
  ],
  "unresolved": [
   {
    "id": "identifier",
    "text": "question left open"
   }
  ]
 },
 "evidence_use": "how the retrieved information (or its absence) changed your assessment",
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}

Risk codes (use only these): SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER, EXPOSURE_SPARSITY, MEASURE_RELIABILITY, RTM_SELECTION, OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL, LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD, MECHANICAL_FLOOR, BASE_RATE, CONFOUNDING, LABEL_ERROR, PLACEBO_DEPENDENCE, MULTIPLE_TESTING, IMPLEMENTATION_BUG, DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY
Field guidance:
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

messages.content:
{
 "action": "tool",
 "tool": "retrieve_history",
 "args": {
  "research_question": "Is the PULL_UP shot a league-wide counter-strategy to failed Pick and Roll (PNR) possessions, demonstrating a positive 'lift' (improvement in points per possession) in at least 70% of units, with a median lift greater than 2 points per possession?",
  "query": "PNR fail PULL_UP counter strategy lift points per possession defensive suppression"
 }
}

{
 "research_question": "Is the PULL_UP shot a league-wide counter-strategy to failed Pick and Roll (PNR) possessions, demonstrating a positive 'lift' (improvement in points per possession) in at least 70% of units, with a median lift greater than 2 points per possession?",
 "retrieval_summary": {
  "prior_findings": [
   {
    "id": "RES:CHT-008",
    "status": "descriptive",
    "summary": "PULL_UP shot initiates 15.8% of possessions."
   },
   {
    "id": "FIND:SF-APPA-063",
    "status": "supported",
    "summary": "Reported a median lift of +0.4261 PPP with 90.8% positive lift in an unspecified context. This lift is significantly lower than the +2pp target."
   },
   {
    "id": "RES:APPS-011",
    "status": "supported",
    "summary": "Found that PULL_UP PPP increased by +0.301 in playoffs compared to regular season, while PNR PPP decreased by -0.173 in the same context. This suggests PULL_UP can act as a counter, but the magnitude of lift (+0.301 PPP) is much lower than the +2pp target."
   }
  ],
  "failed_experiments": [
   {
    "id": "RES:APPM-058",
    "status": "untestable",
    "summary": "Descriptive experiment to calibrate PNR rates, not designed as a hypothesis test; no pass/fail gate in output."
   },
   {
    "id": "RES:APPS-017",
    "status": "untestable",
    "summary": "Descriptive experiment with a single-game horizon and no outcome comparison."
   }
  ],
  "limitations": [
   {
    "id": "RES:APPS-011",
    "text": "No confidence intervals were reported for PPP changes."
   },
   {
    "id": "RES:APPS-017",
    "text": "Single-game horizon; no outcome comparison."
   }
  ],
  "unresolved": [
   {
    "id": "N/A",
    "text": "The specific hypothesis of PULL_UP as a counter-strategy immediately following a 'failed PNR' with the specified lift thresholds has not been directly tested."
   },
   {
    "id": "FIND:SF-APPA-063",
    "text": "The context of the previously observed median lift of +0.4261 PPP is not specified, making direct comparison to the current research question's target difficult."
   }
  ]
 },
 "evidence_use": "The retrieved information indicates that PULL_UP shots are a common play type (RES:CHT-008) and can exhibit positive lift in certain contexts (RES:APPS-011, FIND:SF-APPA-063). RES:APPS-011 specifically shows PULL_UP lift (+0.301 PPP) concurrent with PNR decline, providing conceptual support for a counter-strategy. However, the observed lifts (+0.301 PPP and +0.4261 PPP) are significantly lower than the +2 PPP target specified in the research question, suggesting the target might be overly ambitious or that the context of 'PNR fail' might yield different results. The absence of direct prior work on 'PNR fail' as a trigger for 'PULL_UP' as a counter means the core question remains unanswered.",
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": 0.8,
  "blocking_constraints": []
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "no",
  "cited": [
   "RES:CHT-008",
   "FIND:SF-APPA-063",
   "RES:APPS-011"
  ],
  "summary": "Previous work confirms PULL_UP as a common play type and shows it can exhibit positive lift (0.3-0.4 PPP) in some contexts, including when PNR performance declines. However, the specific scenario of PULL_UP as a counter after a 'failed PNR' with a median lift greater than 2 PPP for 70% of units has not been studied. The observed lifts are considerably lower than the target."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
   "text": "Identifying sequences of 'failed PNR' immediately followed by 'PULL_UP' for specific 'units' (e.g., 5-man lineups or individual players) might result in sparse data, especially for less frequent combinations, impacting statistical power and reliability of lift estimates per unit."
  },
  {
   "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
   "text": "Points Per Possession (PPP) calculations can be highly noisy for low numbers of possessions, leading to unreliable 'lift' estimates for units with infrequent occurrences of the specified sequence."
  },
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "Other unmeasured factors (e.g., defensive scheme changes, shot clock situation, specific player matchups, coaching instructions) might influence the decision to take a PULL_UP shot after a PNR, rather than solely the 'failure' of the PNR, potentially confounding the observed lift."
  },
  {
   "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
   "text": "The accuracy of identifying 'PNR fail' and 'PULL_UP' from derived play-type possession records is critical. Errors in these labels could lead to an inaccurate assessment of the counter-strategy."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "Given the ambitious target of a median lift > 2 PPP and positive lift in >=70% of units, there is a risk of insufficient statistical power to detect such an effect if the true lift is smaller or the number of relevant 'failed PNR -> PULL_UP' sequences is low."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.1,
  "rejected": 0.4,
  "untestable": 0.1,
  "artifact": 0.1,
  "uncertain": 0.3
 },
 "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The primary estimand is the median 'lift' in Points Per Possession (PPP) for PULL_UP possessions immediately following a 'failed PNR' across all relevant units. 'Lift' is defined as the difference between the PPP of such PULL_UP possessions and the baseline PPP of PULL_UP possessions not immediately following a failed PNR. A secondary estimand is the proportion of units (individual players) exhibiting a positive lift.",
  "unit": "Individual player as the initiator of the PULL_UP shot. A 'unit' for this analysis will be defined as an individual player who attempts a PULL_UP shot after a failed PNR.",
  "sample": "All PULL_UP possessions from the NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 (complete), and 2025-26 (complete) regular seasons and playoffs that immediately follow a 'failed PNR' possession initiated by the same player. A 'failed PNR' is defined as a PNR possession that results in a turnover, a shot with an Expected Points value below 0.8 PPP, or a shot clock violation, without a foul drawn. A minimum threshold of 50 'failed PNR -> PULL_UP' sequences per player will be required for inclusion in the 'unit' analysis to mitigate mechanical floor effects.",
  "power": "A power analysis will be conducted to determine the minimum number of 'failed PNR -> PULL_UP' sequences required to detect a median lift of 2 PPP with 80% power at alpha=0.05, assuming a standard deviation of PPP consistent with historical data (e.g., 0.6-0.8 PPP). Given the observed lifts in prior work are much lower, the feasibility of detecting a 2 PPP lift will be critically assessed, and if deemed unfeasible, the target lift will be adjusted to a more realistic value (e.g., 0.5 PPP) while still reporting on the 2 PPP threshold.",
  "controls": "To control for confounding factors, the analysis will include controls for game state (score differential, quarter/period), opponent defensive rating, and the player's overall PULL_UP PPP baseline. A matched-pair or regression-based approach will be considered to compare PULL_UP PPP after failed PNRs to PULL_UP PPP in other contexts, controlling for these variables.",
  "falsification": "A falsification test will compare the lift of PULL_UP shots following a 'failed PNR' to the lift of PULL_UP shots following a 'successful PNR' (e.g., PNR possessions resulting in >1.2 PPP). If PULL_UP after successful PNRs shows similar or greater lift, it would undermine the 'counter-strategy' hypothesis. Additionally, a similar analysis will be performed for other common play types (e.g., DRIVE, SPOT_UP) following a failed PNR to ascertain if PULL_UP exhibits a uniquely high lift.",
  "multiplicity": "The analysis will involve evaluating lift for multiple individual players ('units'). A False Discovery Rate (FDR) correction (e.g., Benjamini-Hochberg procedure) will be applied to p-values when assessing the proportion of units showing a positive lift to account for multiple comparisons.",
  "decision_rule": "The hypothesis will be supported if: 1) The league-wide median lift in PPP for PULL_UP shots immediately following a 'failed PNR' is greater than 2 PPP. 2) At least 70% of individual players (units) show a statistically significant positive lift (p < 0.05 after FDR correction) in their PULL_UP PPP in this context. If the 2 PPP target is deemed unfeasible, a revised, more realistic target (e.g., 0.5 PPP) will be used for primary decision, with the 2 PPP target still reported as a high bar.",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2023-24 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2024-25 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2025-26 regular season and playoffs (all complete)."
 }
}

